  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 116 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nNrn Rockies wave will push a strong cold front south through the\narea Mon night, with boundary near the NM border by Tue morning.\nMuch cooler air mass will cover the plains during the day Tue,\nwith max temps 15-20 degf cooler than Mon at many locations.\nOver the mountains, slightly more moisture rotates north out of\nNM into the area, so expect an upturn in shower/thunderstorm\ncoverage over much of the high terrain and interior valleys by\nafternoon. On the plains, air mass may be too cool/stable for\nmuch convection during the day, then increasing chances for\nshowers/storms Tue evening as mountain convection moves e-ne, \nand storms along the old cold frontal boundary just south of the\nborder potentially shift newd as well. \n\nNice monsoonal tap of moisture develops Wed-Sat, with strong\ntrough along the West Coast and upper high over the southeastern\nU.S. Expect widespread showers/thunderstorms each day through\nthe period, with heavy rainfall threat increasing, especially\nover area burn scars. May start to see some drying on Sun as\nwestern trough moves east and deeper moisture is shunted\neastward, though refreshed post-frontal upslope behind potential\nweekend cold front may keep storms going over at least the ern\nmountains and plains. Temps will drift back back upward a few \ndegf Wed-Fri, before potential cold front drops readings back \nbelow average for Sat/Sun.",
